// NEGATIVE TEST — a non-exhaustive match is a compile error for EVERY scrutinee
// type, not only for enums (N6-10).
//
// The spec's sentence is one sentence: "`match` is exhaustive: a non-exhaustive
// match is a compile error, not a silent fall-through, and this applies to every
// scrutinee type, not only enums." Enums were checked from the start and `bool`
// since N6-02; every other type was an UNCHECKED position, so this program
// compiled, fell through all of its arms at run time, and left the value it
// binds holding whatever the temporary was initialised to.
//
// NO INTERVAL ARITHMETIC IS PROMISED, and the diagnostic says so. `0..=59` beside
// `60..=<i64 max>` beside `<i64 min>..=-1` covers every integer, and this checker
// still refuses it: proving that is arithmetic over intervals, and a checker that
// did it for `i64` would owe the same for every other unenumerable type. The rule
// is the simple one — some arm must match every value — and the fix is a `_` or a
// binding arm.
fn main() {
    let v = match 4 {
        1 => 10,
        2 => 20,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
